From 2c470d364819ce7c4be4d09800e6243726e552fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 08:22:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Three rules: subrc_after_assign, preferred_parameter_ignored,
 delete_index_in_loop

subrc_after_assign (warning). A sy-subrc test whose nearest preceding
sy-subrc-setting statement is a plain ASSIGN. On 7.40 SP7 a successful ASSIGN
does not reset sy-subrc, so the test reads whatever the last statement that
DOES set it left there - abap2UI5#1937 was an endless loop on one customer's
system from exactly this, the value still 4 from a READ TABLE in a branch that
had not been taken. Release-dependent and silent everywhere else, which is the
worst combination.

IS ASSIGNED is not a drop-in replacement everywhere, and the rule says so
rather than offering a fix that would introduce a bug: a FAILED ASSIGN leaves
the field symbol bound to whatever it pointed at before, so inside a loop, or
after an earlier ASSIGN to the same symbol, IS ASSIGNED reads TRUE for a
failure. Those sites are still reported - the sy-subrc read is wrong either
way - with the UNASSIGN the remedy needs named in the message and no quick
fix attached. ASSIGN COMPONENT ... OF STRUCTURE is not reported at all: there
sy-subrc distinguishes "component not found" from "assigned" and is the
documented check.

The quick fix rewrites the comparison in place, so it survives a compound
condition: `IF sy-subrc = 0 AND lv_flag = abap_true.` becomes
`IF <fs> IS ASSIGNED AND lv_flag = abap_true.`

preferred_parameter_ignored (warning). PREFERRED PARAMETER names the importing
parameter a positional call fills, which only means something when a parameter
may be LEFT OUT - so the addition is ignored while any importing parameter is
neither OPTIONAL nor carries a DEFAULT. The quick fix REMOVES the addition
rather than making the parameter optional: the compiler's own wording suggests
the opposite, and that is the more dangerous of the two, because it widens the
contract so a call that forgets the parameter compiles.

delete_index_in_loop. DELETE itab INDEX sy-tabix inside a LOOP AT that same
table: sy-tabix is the loop's own cursor, so the delete shortens the table
under the walk - the next row is skipped and the last round raises
TABLE_INVALID_INDEX. Anchored on the ENCLOSING LOOP rather than on the
statement, because `DELETE itab INDEX sy-tabix` right after `READ TABLE itab`
is the standard read-then-delete and is correct; a rule that reported the
shape unconditionally would be switched off within a week.

Measured on four real code bases (abap2UI5, abap2UI5/samples,
abap2UI5/samples-controls, abap2UI5/samples-stack - 1054 files):

  subrc_after_assign            66  (28 / 14 / 22 / 2)
  delete_index_in_loop           2  the vendored ajson filter lib, and one
                                    port - both real, both the shape above
  preferred_parameter_ignored    0  those code bases run an ATC gate for it
                                    already, so the rule has fixture evidence
                                    only

  npm test (packages/core)   11020 passing, eslint clean, schema regenerated

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WNnsZnJAf6zEg8phCFVoB5
